c1b9e3be29d2bb053ba9029`, 475,141 B, LV2026 version bytes unchanged, original byte-identical. First one this project has made, and the cycle's saved artefact.\n- The mandatory failed-prediction review ($4.24, opus/max) refuted my benign reading and named `claudeDev\\background VIs_COPY\\` as the hazard. Its own discriminating test then **inverted the finding**: the saved copy matches the ORIGINAL on all 98 SubVI rows, while the *pristine byte copy* re-binds 22 calls into that directory and loses 8. So `shutil.copy2(ORIGINAL, claudeDev\\…)` has been producing silently re-bound VIs all along, and gate B had been comparing the artefact against the defective side.\n\nDecisions recorded as Pre-decided 29(a)–(j): preload-then-save permitted for `claudeDev` stage artefacts, `allow_broken=True` banned, stage boundaries must fall at legal states, and every stage gate compares to the ORIGINAL. Two repairs applied — `SAVE_ALLOWLIST = set()` (it contained an *original*, a live rule-1 hole) and the stop-record command-position exemption that broke a deadlock two archived reviews had left to judgement.\n\n**What I did not deliver:** `D1_s1_copy.vi` does not exist. Gate B's wrong reference stopped the first run; the deadlock ate an act; then the regression self-test that repair required consumed the last build-budget slot, and clearing that budget requires the retrospective, which ends the session. The run is patched, statically audited 6/0, and gate-clear — it is the next session's first act, with the exact command in `## NEXT`.\n\n**Flagged for you (rule 2c):** Pre-decided 29 narrows your 16(b) to permit a preload-then-save, and S0's ops were accepted on a completed G-C. Both are my reading of your own rules; only you can overturn them.","ttft_ms":1997,"type":"result","duration_ms":7414269,"uuid":"c569ce69-d4a1-453f-85a5-998a826bb942","ttft_stream_ms":1600,"time_to_request_ms":156,"queued_turn_count":0}
